WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.967
Hello, everyone! I'm Mr. Daniel. Welcome to Online Upper-Intermediate.

2
00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:08.340
Hi! I'm Nita. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:14.450
Unit seven: Career Moves. In this unit, you will discuss career decisions and their consequences.

4
00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:16.242
headhunt.

5
00:00:16.942 --> 00:00:20.822
headhunt means to find and offer a job to someone who works for another company.

6
00:00:21.122 --> 00:00:23.308
She was headhunted by a big bank.

7
00:00:24.008 --> 00:00:26.080
Your turn. Say: headhunt.

8
00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:29.583
headhunt.

9
00:00:30.583 --> 00:00:31.445
resign.

10
00:00:32.145 --> 00:00:34.458
resign means to officially leave your job.

11
00:00:34.758 --> 00:00:36.897
He resigned to start his own business.

12
00:00:37.597 --> 00:00:39.913
Your turn. Say: resign.

13
00:00:42.613 --> 00:00:43.567
resign.

14
00:00:44.583 --> 00:00:45.492
job offer.

15
00:00:46.192 --> 00:00:49.027
job offer means an offer of employment from a company.

16
00:00:49.327 --> 00:00:51.408
I received two job offers in one week!

17
00:00:52.108 --> 00:00:54.296
Your turn. Say: job offer.

18
00:00:56.996 --> 00:00:58.032
job offer.

19
00:00:59.017 --> 00:00:59.832
perks.

20
00:01:00.532 --> 00:01:03.158
perks means extra benefits you get from your job.

21
00:01:03.458 --> 00:01:06.155
Free lunch and gym membership are nice perks.

22
00:01:06.855 --> 00:01:09.008
Your turn. Say: perks.

23
00:01:11.708 --> 00:01:12.593
perks.

24
00:01:13.583 --> 00:01:14.654
career ladder.

25
00:01:15.354 --> 00:01:19.258
career ladder means the series of higher positions you can reach in your career.

26
00:01:19.558 --> 00:01:21.476
She climbed the career ladder quickly.

27
00:01:22.176 --> 00:01:24.410
Your turn. Say: career ladder.

28
00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:28.320
career ladder.

29
00:01:29.317 --> 00:01:30.817
promotion prospects.

30
00:01:31.517 --> 00:01:35.130
promotion prospects means the chances of getting a higher position.

31
00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:38.115
This company has excellent promotion prospects.

32
00:01:38.815 --> 00:01:41.607
Your turn. Say: promotion prospects.

33
00:01:44.307 --> 00:01:45.946
promotion prospects.

34
00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:47.812
job hunting.

35
00:01:48.512 --> 00:01:50.755
job hunting means looking for a new job.

36
00:01:51.055 --> 00:01:52.985
Job hunting can take several months.

37
00:01:53.685 --> 00:01:56.430
Your turn. Say: job hunting.

38
00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.213
job hunting.

39
00:02:01.217 --> 00:02:02.078
recruiter.

40
00:02:02.778 --> 00:02:05.985
recruiter means a person who finds new staff for companies.

41
00:02:06.285 --> 00:02:08.424
A recruiter contacted me on LinkedIn.

42
00:02:09.124 --> 00:02:11.800
Your turn. Say: recruiter.

43
00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.454
recruiter.

44
00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:18.357
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:18.657 --> 00:02:23.480
A recruiter called me yesterday. A tech company wants to headhunt me!

46
00:02:24.783 --> 00:02:27.958
Congratulations! Will you resign from your current job?

47
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:34.561
I'm not sure. If I hadn't joined this company, I wouldn't have so much experience now.

48
00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:38.526
True. What are the perks of the new job offer?

49
00:02:39.817 --> 00:02:42.768
A higher salary and better promotion prospects.

50
00:02:44.083 --> 00:02:48.242
If I were you, I'd think carefully about the career ladder in both places.

51
00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:52.084
Grammar focus: Mixed conditionals.

52
00:02:52.384 --> 00:02:54.743
Mixed conditionals connect different times.

53
00:02:55.043 --> 00:03:00.688
If plus had plus past participle, then would plus verb, for a past action with a present result.

54
00:03:00.988 --> 00:03:04.787
For example: If I had studied IT, I would be a programmer now.

55
00:03:05.087 --> 00:03:11.638
Mixed conditionals connect different times: a past action with a present result, or a present fact with a past result.

56
00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:15.326
Now read and listen. Should You Stay or Should You Go?.

57
00:03:15.626 --> 00:03:19.622
The average worker today changes jobs every three to four years.

58
00:03:20.917 --> 00:03:25.169
Many people start job hunting when they feel their promotion prospects are limited.

59
00:03:26.483 --> 00:03:29.876
Recruiters often headhunt talented employees with attractive perks.

60
00:03:31.183 --> 00:03:35.331
However, experts warn that resigning too quickly can damage your reputation.

61
00:03:36.617 --> 00:03:39.684
If you had stayed longer in each job, you might be more senior now.

62
00:03:40.983 --> 00:03:43.427
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

63
00:03:43.727 --> 00:03:45.912
To officially leave your job is to blank.

64
00:03:46.212 --> 00:03:49.436
A, headhunt. B, resign. C, recruit.

65
00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:55.763
The answer is B: resign.

66
00:03:57.250 --> 00:03:59.459
If I had saved more, I blank a house now.

67
00:03:59.759 --> 00:04:02.855
A, would own. B, would have owned. C, will own.

68
00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:08.733
The answer is A: would own.

69
00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.354
If he were braver, he blank the job.

70
00:04:12.654 --> 00:04:16.435
A, would accept. B, would have accepted. C, accepts.

71
00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:22.566
The answer is B: would have accepted.

72
00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:26.433
Extra benefits from your job are blank.

73
00:04:26.733 --> 00:04:30.525
A, perks. B, job offers. C, career ladders.

74
00:04:34.517 --> 00:04:36.307
The answer is A: perks.

75
00:04:37.817 --> 00:04:41.593
According to the text, the average worker changes jobs every blank.

76
00:04:41.893 --> 00:04:45.337
A, one year. B, three to four years. C, ten years.

77
00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:51.837
The answer is B: three to four years.

78
00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:58.150
Now it's your turn to speak. If you had chosen a different career, how would your life be different now?

79
00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:09.525
If I had studied medicine, I would be working in a hospital now, and I'd probably have less free time.

80
00:05:09.825 --> 00:05:11.523
Excellent answer, Nita!

81
00:05:12.517 --> 00:05:16.260
Great work today! You learned eight new words and mixed conditionals.

82
00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:19.874
Today's value is hard work. Work hard and never give up.

83
00:05:20.174 --> 00:05:22.316
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

84
00:05:22.616 --> 00:05:25.094
Thank you, Mr. Daniel! Bye, everyone!

